<section id="S5.SS3.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">ModelNet-C</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ ScanObjectNN ‣ 5.3 Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ SODA: Out-of-Distribution Detection in Domain-Shifted Point Clouds via Neighborhood Propagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the average performance with ModelNet-C test samples. For brevity, we show the average results over both experimental setups for ZS-SODA and SODA, followed by the change in these metrics from the initial scores before score propagation (in parentheses). We see that our methodology consistently improves performance in both metrics across corruption types, except for ZS-SODA which declines slightly for the jitter corruption. On average, we see a <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.8</annotation></semantics></math> point improvement in AUC for both ZS-SODA and SODA, and a <math id="S5.SS3.SSSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="-8.0/-10.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>8.0</mn><mo rspace="0em">/</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>10.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-8.0/-10.3</annotation></semantics></math> improvement in FPR95 scores for ZS-SODA/SODA. The full results are shown in the supplementary.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Ablation Study</h3>

<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S5.F5.2" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:110.4pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.21892v1/T.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:153/150;" width="153" height="150"></object>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S5.F5.3" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:110.4pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.21892v1/alpha.svg" id="S5.F5.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:153/153;" width="153" height="153"></object>
</div></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<div id="S5.F5.4" class="ltx_block ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:110.4pt;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.21892v1/eta.svg" id="S5.F5.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:153/158;" width="153" height="158"></object>
</div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S5.F5.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Average AUC performance with different numbers of iterations, <math id="S5.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> (left), settings of <math id="S5.F5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> (middle), and settings of <math id="S5.F5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> (right).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Hyper-parameters</h4>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S5.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 5.4 Ablation Study ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ SODA: Out-of-Distribution Detection in Domain-Shifted Point Clouds via Neighborhood Propagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows how performance varies with different settings of hyper-parameters. Firstly, the leftmost figure shows AUC performance after different numbers of propagation iterations. <math id="S5.SS4.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=0</annotation></semantics></math>, gives the initial score, from which we see a large increase in performance after just one iteration. Performance slightly drops before converging at around <math id="S5.SS4.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=6</annotation></semantics></math> iterations, after which the OOD scores are fully converged and there is no more change in performance. In the middle figure, we see how performance changes as <math id="S5.SS4.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. <a href="#S4.E4" title="In Score Propagation ‣ 4.2 SODA ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ SODA: Out-of-Distribution Detection in Domain-Shifted Point Clouds via Neighborhood Propagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> varies. <math id="S5.SS4.SSSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math> is equivalent to the initial scores, while a smaller <math id="S5.SS4.SSSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> gives greater weight to the current score information from neighbors in score updates. We see that optimal performance is achieved around <math id="S5.SS4.SSSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\approx 0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\approx 0.25</annotation></semantics></math>, which signifies the importance of neighborhood information in refining the initial scores. The rightmost figure shows performance for different settings of <math id="S5.SS4.SSSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>. A larger <math id="S5.SS4.SSSx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to a lower <math id="S5.SS4.SSSx1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> threshold in Eq. <a href="#S4.E3" title="In Score Propagation ‣ 4.2 SODA ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ SODA: Out-of-Distribution Detection in Domain-Shifted Point Clouds via Neighborhood Propagation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, and consequently a denser similarity graph with more connecting edges between samples. We see peak performance is achieved at <math id="S5.SS4.SSSx1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\approx 0.015" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.015</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\approx 0.015</annotation></semantics></math>, and slowly declines from there. As <math id="S5.SS4.SSSx1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> increases, there are more samples propagating score information to neighbors of lower cosine similarity, which eventually hinders performance as the propagated information becomes less relevant. In practice, the setting of these hyper-parameters are guided by the number of available samples and also the sensitivity of individual applications to the trade-off between false positive and negative errors.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
